## Background

Type 2 diabetes is associated with several metabolic and
anthropometric risk factors, including obesity, glucose regulation,
blood pressure, and lipid profiles.

Because these variables may be correlated, analysing them independently
does not fully describe their joint structure. Principal Component
Analysis (PCA) can reduce a multivariate dataset into a smaller number
of components that capture the major patterns of variation.

## Aim

The aim of this analysis is to explore the multivariate structure of
the diabetes dataset using Principal Component Analysis (PCA).

Specifically, we aim to:

1. Determine how much variation is captured by the principal
   components.
2. Identify which metabolic and anthropometric variables contribute
   most strongly to the first principal components.
3. Explore whether patterns in PCA space are associated with BMI class
   and biological sex.

## Load Libraries

```{r}
#| message: false
#| warning: false

library("tidyverse")
library("broom")
```

## Load Data

The diabetes dataset was obtained from the Vanderbilt University
Department of Biostatistics.

```{r}
raw_dir <- "data/_raw/"
data_file <- "diabetes.csv"

data_url <- str_c(
  "https://hbiostat.org/data/repo/",
  data_file
)

if (!dir.exists(raw_dir)) {
  dir.create(
    path = raw_dir,
    recursive = TRUE
  )
}

data_path <- str_c(
  raw_dir,
  data_file
)

if (!file.exists(data_path)) {
  download.file(
    url = data_url,
    destfile = data_path
  )
}

diabetes_raw <- read_csv(
  file = data_path,
  show_col_types = FALSE
)
```

## Data Preparation

BMI was calculated from weight in pounds and height in inches. BMI
classes were defined to facilitate interpretation of the PCA scores.

```{r}
diabetes <- diabetes_raw |>
  filter(
    !is.na(height),
    !is.na(weight)
  ) |>
  mutate(
    bmi = weight / height^2 * 703,
    bmi_class = case_when(
      bmi < 18.5 ~ "Underweight",
      bmi < 25 ~ "Normal weight",
      bmi < 30 ~ "Overweight",
      bmi >= 30 ~ "Obese"
    ),
    bmi_class = factor(
      bmi_class,
      levels = c(
        "Underweight",
        "Normal weight",
        "Overweight",
        "Obese"
      )
    ),
    gender = factor(gender)
  )
```

For the PCA, we selected variables describing age, body composition,
blood lipids, glucose regulation, and blood pressure.

Observations containing missing values in any of the selected PCA
variables were removed.

```{r}
diabetes_pca_data <- diabetes |>
  select(
    gender,
    bmi_class,
    age,
    bmi,
    waist,
    hip,
    chol,
    stab.glu,
    hdl,
    glyhb,
    bp.1s,
    bp.1d
  ) |>
  drop_na()
```

```{r}
diabetes_pca_data |>
  summarise(
    n_individuals = n()
  )
```

## Exploratory Overview

Before performing PCA, the distribution of BMI classes was examined.

```{r}
diabetes_pca_data |>
  count(
    gender,
    bmi_class
  ) |>
  group_by(gender) |>
  mutate(
    proportion = n / sum(n)
  ) |>
  ungroup() |>
  ggplot(
    aes(
      x = gender,
      y = proportion,
      fill = bmi_class
    )
  ) +
  geom_col() +
  scale_y_continuous(
    labels = scales::percent_format()
  ) +
  labs(
    x = "Sex",
    y = "Proportion",
    fill = "BMI class",
    title = "BMI Class Distribution by Sex"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

## Principal Component Analysis

Only numerical variables were included when fitting the PCA.

Because the variables are measured on different scales, they were
centered and scaled before calculating the principal components.

```{r}
pca_input <- diabetes_pca_data |>
  select(
    age,
    bmi,
    waist,
    hip,
    chol,
    stab.glu,
    hdl,
    glyhb,
    bp.1s,
    bp.1d
  )

pca_fit <- pca_input |>
  prcomp(
    center = TRUE,
    scale. = TRUE
  )
```

## Variance Explained

The amount of total variation explained by each principal component was
extracted using `broom::tidy()`.

```{r}
pca_variance <- pca_fit |>
  tidy(
    matrix = "eigenvalues"
  )

pca_variance
```

```{r}
pca_variance |>
  ggplot(
    aes(
      x = PC,
      y = percent
    )
  ) +
  geom_col() +
  scale_x_continuous(
    breaks = pca_variance$PC
  ) +
  scale_y_continuous(
    labels = scales::percent_format(),
    expand = expansion(
      mult = c(0, 0.02)
    )
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Variance Explained by Each Principal Component",
    x = "Principal component",
    y = "Variance explained"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

```{r}
pc1_variance <- pca_variance |>
  filter(
    PC == 1
  ) |>
  pull(percent)

pc2_variance <- pca_variance |>
  filter(
    PC == 2
  ) |>
  pull(percent)
```

PC1 explained
`r scales::percent(pc1_variance, accuracy = 0.1)` of the total
variation, while PC2 explained
`r scales::percent(pc2_variance, accuracy = 0.1)`.

Together, the first two principal components explained
`r scales::percent(pc1_variance + pc2_variance, accuracy = 0.1)` of
the total variation in the selected variables.

## PCA Scores

The original participant information was combined with the PCA scores
using `augment()`.

```{r}
pca_scores <- pca_fit |>
  augment(
    data = diabetes_pca_data
  )
```

The first two principal components were visualised while indicating BMI
class and sex.

```{r}
pca_scores |>
  ggplot(
    aes(
      x = .fittedPC1,
      y = .fittedPC2,
      colour = bmi_class,
      shape = gender
    )
  ) +
  geom_point(
    alpha = 0.75,
    size = 2
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Individuals in Principal Component Space",
    subtitle = "Colour indicates BMI class and shape indicates sex",
    x = "PC1",
    y = "PC2",
    colour = "BMI class",
    shape = "Sex"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

This plot can be used to assess whether individuals with similar BMI
classes or sex tend to occupy similar regions of the multivariate
principal-component space.

## PCA Loadings

Loadings describe how strongly each original variable contributes to
each principal component.

```{r}
pca_loadings <- pca_fit |>
  tidy(
    matrix = "rotation"
  )

pca_loadings
```

The variables with the largest absolute loadings on PC1 and PC2 were
identified.

```{r}
top_loadings <- pca_loadings |>
  filter(
    PC %in% c(1, 2)
  ) |>
  group_by(PC) |>
  slice_max(
    order_by = abs(value),
    n = 4,
    with_ties = FALSE
  ) |>
  ungroup()

top_loadings
```

To visualise how the variables contribute simultaneously to PC1 and
PC2, the rotation matrix was converted to wide format.

```{r}
pca_loadings_wide <- pca_loadings |>
  filter(
    PC %in% c(1, 2)
  ) |>
  mutate(
    PC = str_c(
      "PC",
      PC
    )
  ) |>
  pivot_wider(
    names_from = PC,
    values_from = value
  )
```

```{r}
arrow_style <- grid::arrow(
  angle = 20,
  ends = "last",
  type = "closed",
  length = grid::unit(
    5,
    "pt"
  )
)

pca_loadings_wide |>
  ggplot(
    aes(
      x = PC1,
      y = PC2
    )
  ) +
  geom_hline(
    yintercept = 0,
    linetype = "dashed"
  ) +
  geom_vline(
    xintercept = 0,
    linetype = "dashed"
  ) +
  geom_segment(
    aes(
      x = 0,
      y = 0,
      xend = PC1,
      yend = PC2
    ),
    arrow = arrow_style
  ) +
  geom_text(
    aes(
      label = column
    ),
    nudge_x = 0.025,
    nudge_y = 0.025,
    check_overlap = TRUE
  ) +
  coord_fixed() +
  labs(
    title = "Variable Loadings on PC1 and PC2",
    x = "PC1 loading",
    y = "PC2 loading"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

Variables represented by longer arrows have stronger contributions to
the first two principal components. Variables pointing in similar
directions tend to be positively associated, whereas variables
pointing in opposite directions show opposing contributions.

## Conclusion

PCA provided a lower-dimensional representation of the metabolic and
anthropometric variables in the diabetes dataset.

The variance-explained plot shows how much of the total multivariate
variation is retained by each component, while the loading plot
identifies the variables that contribute most strongly to PC1 and PC2.

The PCA score plot additionally allows the multivariate profiles of
individuals to be compared according to BMI class and sex. Any apparent
group separation should be interpreted descriptively, because PCA is
an unsupervised exploratory method rather than a formal hypothesis
test.

Overall, the analysis demonstrates how PCA can be integrated into a
tidyverse workflow to explore relationships among multiple correlated
biological and clinical variables.

